EXT. FISHING TRAWLER "MARGIT ANN" - DECK - DAWN

Grey sea. Grey sky. Gulls wheel overhead.

Two deckhands haul nets, whistling. A third coils rope and is bored out of his mind. Everything is fine.

Captain Roy Dunmore, 50s, leans on the rail with a mug, watching the horizon. Beside him, Dr. Anika Rao, 30s, sits on an upturned crate, clipboard on her knee. She is the ship's medic by contract and by accident.

ANIKA
Quietest trip I've had in months.

ROY
Don't say that.

ANIKA
Why not?

ROY
You know why not.

The deckhands laugh at something. A gull lands on the winch.

WHAM.

A net swings loose. It slams into the deckhand's back. He goes down hard.

TEO, 20s, a deckhand, lies on the planks, face grey, one arm pinned.

ANIKA
Don't move him!

ROY
Get the line off him!

The two deckhands scramble. The gulls scatter. Roy and Anika drop to their knees at the same time and almost collide.

ANIKA
Teo. Teo, look at me. Can you feel your hands?

TEO
(strained)
No.

ANIKA
Okay. That's okay. Roy, I need the board and I need it flat.

ROY
Board's in the wheelhouse. Two minutes.

ANIKA
You don't have two minutes.

ROY
Then tell me what to do.

ANIKA
Hold his head. Gently. Like it's glass.

Roy does. Anika's hands move fast, over the neck, the ribs, the pulse.

TEO
Doc. Am I

ANIKA
You're not going anywhere. I promise.

ROY
(low)
You can't promise that.

ANIKA
Watch me.

This is the last time any of them will take the sea for granted.

The deckhands return with the board. Together, they roll him.
